<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.behalfbot.colima.plist - chassis-rendered template.

  Generated from chassis/launchd/com.behalfbot.colima.plist.template by
  chassis/scripts/bootstrap-customer-scripts.sh.

  THE SINGLE OWNER of Colima startup on a macOS install. Nothing else on the
  box may run `colima start`. If another job needs Docker, it WAITS for the
  socket - see the wait loop at the top of
  chassis/scripts/templates/restart-discord.sh.template for the pattern.

  DOMAIN: LaunchDaemon (/Library/LaunchDaemons/, system domain). This is one of
  the few jobs docs/launchd-domains.md actually clears for the daemon side: it
  touches no login keychain, runs no `claude`, and shares no tmux server. It is
  pure container-lifecycle infrastructure, and it must come up on an unattended
  reboot with nobody logged in - which is exactly what a gui LaunchAgent cannot
  do. Do NOT convert this one to an agent.

  WHY IT POINTS AT A WRAPPER AND NOT AT `colima start`
  (scrollinondubs/new-jaxity#550, 2026-09-05)

  The plist this replaces ran `colima start -f` directly. After a hard
  power-off, Lima leaves stale ha.pid / vz.pid / ha.sock / ssh.sock behind and
  the profile comes back Broken; `colima start` then dies on every boot with

      errors inspecting instance: [failed to get Info from ".../ha.sock":
      dial unix .../ha.sock: connect: connection refused]

  A bare `colima start` can never clear that. The wrapper runs
  `colima stop --force` (which removes the stale files and is non-destructive -
  it does not touch the VM disk image) and retries once, then verifies
  `docker ps` actually answers before reporting success.

  WHY RunAtLoad + StartInterval AND NOT KeepAlive

  The old plist carried KeepAlive { SuccessfulExit: true }, which tells launchd
  to respawn the job when it exits ZERO and to leave it alone when it fails. So
  the one case that needed a retry - a failed boot start - was the one case
  launchd never retried, and the job sat parked at exit status 1 while Docker
  stayed down. Its comment claimed the opposite.

  colima-ensure.sh is one-shot and idempotent instead: it exits 0 immediately
  when `docker ps` already answers. A 5-minute StartInterval therefore gives
  both boot recovery and continuous self-heal for a few milliseconds a tick,
  with none of the hot-loop risk of KeepAlive-on-failure against a VM that
  genuinely cannot start.

  WHY AbandonProcessGroup

  The plist this replaces ran `colima start -f`, so the colima process stayed
  resident for as long as the VM did and launchd had something to supervise.
  This one exits as soon as Docker answers, and launchd SIGKILLs a job's
  remaining process group on exit. If the Lima hostagent shared this wrapper's
  process group, the daemon would start a VM and kill it again every tick.

  It does not. Verified on colima 0.10.1 / Lima, reading the pids Lima itself
  records:

      $ ps -o pid,pgid,ppid,comm -p $(cat ~/.colima/_lima/colima/ha.pid)
        PID  PGID  PPID  COMM
       8850  8850     1  /opt/homebrew/bin/limactl

  PGID equals PID and PPID is 1: the hostagent detaches into its own process
  group and reparents to init, so it was never in our group to begin with. The
  same holds for the colima daemon and the ssh mux. AbandonProcessGroup is set
  anyway, as insurance against a future Colima that stops detaching - the cost
  is that launchd stops tracking children we explicitly want to outlive us,
  which is precisely the intent here.

  The same fact clears the migration: booting out the old resident `-f` daemon
  during activation does not take a running VM down with it.

  WHY UserName rather than root

  Colima VM state lives in ${USER}'s home (~/.colima, ~/.lima) and the docker
  socket is created under it. A root-owned Colima would either refuse those
  files or stand up a parallel root-owned VM that every existing `docker`
  invocation would miss. UserName keeps the ownership invariants intact.
  `HOME` is set explicitly because launchd does not run a login shell.

  WHY THE LABEL IS NOT BOT-SCOPED

  Every other chassis plist is com.behalfbot.<bot>-<name>. This one is
  not, deliberately: Colima is a per-machine singleton, not a per-bot resource.
  Two installs on one Mac share one VM and must share one owner, so they share
  one label. Whichever install activates last owns the plist; because the
  wrapper is idempotent and locked, that is harmless.

  Template variables (expanded at render time):
    CUSTOMER_HOME     per-customer state root
    CHASSIS_HOME      chassis git tree root
    USER              installer's macOS short name
    HOMEBREW_PREFIX   /opt/homebrew on Apple Silicon, /usr/local on Intel
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.behalfbot.colima</string>

    <key>UserName</key>
    <string>${USER}</string>
    <key>GroupName</key>
    <string>staff</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>${CHASSIS_HOME}/chassis/scripts/colima-ensure.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>300</integer>

    <!-- The VM must outlive this one-shot wrapper. See the comment above:
         Lima's hostagent already detaches into its own process group, so this
         is insurance rather than load-bearing. -->
    <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Daemon-domain plist: only the System session is meaningful. -->
    <key>LimitLoadToSessionType</key>
    <array>
        <string>System</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/${USER}</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>${CUSTOMER_HOME}/logs/scheduled/colima-stderr.log</string>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>${CUSTOMER_HOME}/logs/scheduled/colima-stdout.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>${HOMEBREW_PREFIX}/bin:${HOMEBREW_PREFIX}/sbin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/${USER}</string>
        <key>CUSTOMER_HOME</key>
        <string>${CUSTOMER_HOME}</string>
        <key>CHASSIS_HOME</key>
        <string>${CHASSIS_HOME}</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
